const LESSONS = [{"id": "1", "title": "Variables", "html": "<p><strong>Names refer to objects. Assignment binds a name; it does not automatically copy an object.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>count = 3                 # int\nratio = 0.5               # float\nname = &quot;Ada&quot;              # str\nready = True              # bool\nresult = None             # explicit absence of a value\nx, y = 2, 5               # unpacking; lengths must match\nx, y = y, x               # swap; right side evaluated first\nfirst, *rest = [1, 2, 3]   # first=1, rest=[2, 3]</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Names are case-sensitive; use <code>snake_case</code>, constants conventionally <code>UPPER_CASE</code>. Names cannot start with a digit or be keywords. Avoid shadowing <code>list</code>, <code>str</code>, <code>sum</code>, etc.</li>\n<li>Dynamic typing: <code>x = 1; x = &quot;one&quot;</code> is legal. Objects have types; names can be rebound to objects of different types.</li>\n<li>Common immutable types: <code>int</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>bool</code>, <code>str</code>, <code>tuple</code>, <code>frozenset</code>. Mutable: <code>list</code>, <code>dict</code>, <code>set</code>.</li>\n<li>Conversion: <code>int(&quot;12&quot;) == 12</code>, <code>str(12) == &quot;12&quot;</code>, <code>int(3.9) == 3</code> (toward zero).</li>\n<li><code>int(&quot;3.9&quot;)</code> raises <code>ValueError</code>; <code>&quot;3&quot; + 2</code> raises <code>TypeError</code>.</li>\n<li><code>None</code> differs from <code>0</code>, <code>False</code>, and <code>&quot;&quot;</code>. Check it with <code>is None</code>.</li>\n<li><code>==</code> compares values; <code>is</code> compares object identity. Do not use <code>is</code> for numeric/string equality.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>a = [1]\nb = a\nb.append(2)       # a and b both refer to [1, 2]\nb = [9]          # rebinding b does not change a</code></pre>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>a = b = []</code> creates one shared list. Use <code>a, b = [], []</code> for separate lists.</p>", "text": "\n**Names refer to objects. Assignment binds a name; it does not automatically copy an object.**\n\n```python\ncount = 3                 # int\nratio = 0.5               # float\nname = \"Ada\"              # str\nready = True              # bool\nresult = None             # explicit absence of a value\nx, y = 2, 5               # unpacking; lengths must match\nx, y = y, x               # swap; right side evaluated first\nfirst, *rest = [1, 2, 3]   # first=1, rest=[2, 3]\n```\n\n- Names are case-sensitive; use `snake_case`, constants conventionally `UPPER_CASE`. Names cannot start with a digit or be keywords. Avoid shadowing `list`, `str`, `sum`, etc.\n- Dynamic typing: `x = 1; x = \"one\"` is legal. Objects have types; names can be rebound to objects of different types.\n- Common immutable types: `int`, `float`, `bool`, `str`, `tuple`, `frozenset`. Mutable: `list`, `dict`, `set`.\n- Conversion: `int(\"12\") == 12`, `str(12) == \"12\"`, `int(3.9) == 3` (toward zero).\n- `int(\"3.9\")` raises `ValueError`; `\"3\" + 2` raises `TypeError`.\n- `None` differs from `0`, `False`, and `\"\"`. Check it with `is None`.\n- `==` compares values; `is` compares object identity. Do not use `is` for numeric/string equality.\n\n```python\na = [1]\nb = a\nb.append(2)       # a and b both refer to [1, 2]\nb = [9]          # rebinding b does not change a\n```\n\n**Trap:** `a = b = []` creates one shared list. Use `a, b = [], []` for separate lists.\n\n", "minutes": 8}, {"id": "2", "title": "Math and Boolean operators", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Expression</th><th>Result / meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>7 / 2</code></td><td><code>3.5</code> \u2014 true division</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>7 // 2</code></td><td><code>3</code> \u2014 floor division</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>-7 // 2</code></td><td><code>-4</code> \u2014 floors toward negative infinity</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>-7 % 2</code></td><td><code>1</code> \u2014 remainder follows divisor&#x27;s sign</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>2 ** 3</code></td><td><code>8</code> \u2014 exponentiation</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>abs(-4)</code></td><td><code>4</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>divmod(7, 2)</code></td><td><code>(3, 1)</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>a == (a // b) * b + a % b</code> for integers and nonzero <code>b</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Shorthand: <code>+=</code>, <code>-=</code>, <code>*=</code>, <code>//=</code>, <code>%=</code>. Python has no increment operator <code>++</code>.</li>\n<li>Parenthesize when unclear. Arithmetic precedes comparisons; Boolean precedence is <code>not</code>, then <code>and</code>, then <code>or</code>.</li>\n<li><code>and</code> / <code>or</code> short-circuit and <strong>return an operand</strong>, not necessarily a Boolean: <code>0 or 5</code> \u2192 <code>5</code>; <code>&quot;a&quot; and 7</code> \u2192 <code>7</code>.</li>\n<li><code>not x</code> always returns a Boolean. <code>items and items[0]</code> avoids indexing an empty list.</li>\n<li><code>&amp;</code>, <code>|</code>, <code>^</code>, <code>~</code>, <code>&lt;&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;&gt;</code> are bitwise operators on integers; they do not replace Boolean <code>and</code>/<code>or</code>.</li>\n<li>Floats have representation error: <code>0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3</code> is false. For approximate comparison, use <code>math.isclose</code> with suitable tolerances.</li>\n<li>Python integers grow as needed; extremely large integer arithmetic is not constant-time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>+=</code> can mutate a list, but rebinding an integer/string produces a different object.</p>", "text": "\n| Expression | Result / meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `7 / 2` | `3.5` \u2014 true division |\n| `7 // 2` | `3` \u2014 floor division |\n| `-7 // 2` | `-4` \u2014 floors toward negative infinity |\n| `-7 % 2` | `1` \u2014 remainder follows divisor's sign |\n| `2 ** 3` | `8` \u2014 exponentiation |\n| `abs(-4)` | `4` |\n| `divmod(7, 2)` | `(3, 1)` |\n\n`a == (a // b) * b + a % b` for integers and nonzero `b`.\n\n- Shorthand: `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, `//=`, `%=`. Python has no increment operator `++`.\n- Parenthesize when unclear. Arithmetic precedes comparisons; Boolean precedence is `not`, then `and`, then `or`.\n- `and` / `or` short-circuit and **return an operand**, not necessarily a Boolean: `0 or 5` \u2192 `5`; `\"a\" and 7` \u2192 `7`.\n- `not x` always returns a Boolean. `items and items[0]` avoids indexing an empty list.\n- `&`, `|`, `^`, `~`, `<<`, `>>` are bitwise operators on integers; they do not replace Boolean `and`/`or`.\n- Floats have representation error: `0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3` is false. For approximate comparison, use `math.isclose` with suitable tolerances.\n- Python integers grow as needed; extremely large integer arithmetic is not constant-time.\n\n**Trap:** `+=` can mutate a list, but rebinding an integer/string produces a different object.\n\n", "minutes": 8}, {"id": "3", "title": "Functions", "html": "<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>def scaled_total(values: list[int], factor: int = 2) -&gt; int:\n    return sum(values) * factor\n\nanswer = scaled_total([1, 2], factor=3)   # 9</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Parameters are names in the definition; arguments are the supplied values. Arguments can be positional or keyword; required parameters precede ordinary default parameters.</li>\n<li><code>return</code> exits immediately. No return, or bare <code>return</code>, produces <code>None</code>. Printing is not returning.</li>\n<li>Type hints document intent; Python does not enforce them automatically at runtime.</li>\n<li><code>return low, high</code> returns a tuple, which callers can unpack.</li>\n<li>Arguments bind local names to the supplied objects. Mutating a passed list is visible to the caller; rebinding the parameter is not.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>def update(items):\n    items.append(1)  # changes caller&#x27;s list\n    items = []       # only rebinds the local name</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Scope:</strong> Python searches local \u2192 enclosing functions \u2192 global \u2192 built-ins (LEGB). Assignment inside a function normally makes that name local. Reading it before local assignment raises <code>UnboundLocalError</code>. <code>global</code> allows rebinding a module name; <code>nonlocal</code> allows rebinding an enclosing function&#x27;s name. Prefer explicit parameters and returns in interview solutions.</p>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Critical trap: default arguments are evaluated once, when the function is defined.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>def collect(value, items=None):  # avoid items=[]: shared across calls\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(value)\n    return items</code></pre>\n<p>Useful extras: <code>*args</code> collects positional arguments into a tuple; <code>**kwargs</code> collects keyword arguments into a dict. <code>f(*values)</code> unpacks positional arguments; <code>f(**options)</code> unpacks keyword arguments. A small sorting key can use <code>lambda pair: pair[1]</code>.</p>", "text": "\n```python\ndef scaled_total(values: list[int], factor: int = 2) -> int:\n    return sum(values) * factor\n\nanswer = scaled_total([1, 2], factor=3)   # 9\n```\n\n- Parameters are names in the definition; arguments are the supplied values. Arguments can be positional or keyword; required parameters precede ordinary default parameters.\n- `return` exits immediately. No return, or bare `return`, produces `None`. Printing is not returning.\n- Type hints document intent; Python does not enforce them automatically at runtime.\n- `return low, high` returns a tuple, which callers can unpack.\n- Arguments bind local names to the supplied objects. Mutating a passed list is visible to the caller; rebinding the parameter is not.\n\n```python\ndef update(items):\n    items.append(1)  # changes caller's list\n    items = []       # only rebinds the local name\n```\n\n**Scope:** Python searches local \u2192 enclosing functions \u2192 global \u2192 built-ins (LEGB). Assignment inside a function normally makes that name local. Reading it before local assignment raises `UnboundLocalError`. `global` allows rebinding a module name; `nonlocal` allows rebinding an enclosing function's name. Prefer explicit parameters and returns in interview solutions.\n\n**Critical trap: default arguments are evaluated once, when the function is defined.**\n\n```python\ndef collect(value, items=None):  # avoid items=[]: shared across calls\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(value)\n    return items\n```\n\nUseful extras: `*args` collects positional arguments into a tuple; `**kwargs` collects keyword arguments into a dict. `f(*values)` unpacks positional arguments; `f(**options)` unpacks keyword arguments. A small sorting key can use `lambda pair: pair[1]`.\n\n", "minutes": 10}, {"id": "4", "title": "Conditional statements", "html": "<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>if score &gt;= 80:\n    label = &quot;high&quot;\nelif score &gt;= 50:\n    label = &quot;medium&quot;\nelse:\n    label = &quot;low&quot;\n\nsmaller = a if a &lt; b else b</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>An <code>if</code>/<code>elif</code> chain takes only its first true branch. Separate <code>if</code> statements are evaluated independently.</li>\n<li>Comparisons: <code>==</code>, <code>!=</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>; chained comparisons such as <code>0 &lt;= i &lt; n</code> are valid.</li>\n<li>Falsy values include <code>None</code>, <code>False</code>, numeric zero, and empty strings/containers. Most other objects are truthy.</li>\n<li><code>if value</code> treats zero/empty as false; <code>if value is not None</code> permits them.</li>\n<li><code>if</code> and <code>for</code> blocks do <strong>not</strong> introduce a new local scope. A name assigned only in a branch may remain undefined if that branch is skipped.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>if x == 1 or 2:</code> is always truthy. Write <code>if x in (1, 2):</code> or <code>if x == 1 or x == 2:</code>.</p>", "text": "\n```python\nif score >= 80:\n    label = \"high\"\nelif score >= 50:\n    label = \"medium\"\nelse:\n    label = \"low\"\n\nsmaller = a if a < b else b\n```\n\n- An `if`/`elif` chain takes only its first true branch. Separate `if` statements are evaluated independently.\n- Comparisons: `==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`; chained comparisons such as `0 <= i < n` are valid.\n- Falsy values include `None`, `False`, numeric zero, and empty strings/containers. Most other objects are truthy.\n- `if value` treats zero/empty as false; `if value is not None` permits them.\n- `if` and `for` blocks do **not** introduce a new local scope. A name assigned only in a branch may remain undefined if that branch is skipped.\n\n**Trap:** `if x == 1 or 2:` is always truthy. Write `if x in (1, 2):` or `if x == 1 or x == 2:`.\n\n", "minutes": 6}, {"id": "5", "title": "Loops", "html": "<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>for value in values:                 # direct iteration\n    print(value)\nfor i, value in enumerate(values):    # index and value\n    print(i, value)\nfor left, right in zip(xs, ys):       # stops at shorter iterable\n    print(left, right)\n\nfor i in range(2, 8, 2):              # 2, 4, 6; stop excluded\n    print(i)\nfor i in range(3, -1, -1):            # 3, 2, 1, 0\n    print(i)\n\nwhile remaining &gt; 0:\n    remaining -= 1                   # ensure progress toward termination</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>break</code>: exit the innermost loop. <code>continue</code>: skip to its next iteration. <code>pass</code>: do nothing.</li>\n<li><code>reversed(values)</code> iterates backward without making a reversed list.</li>\n<li><code>range</code> stores bounds/step rather than building a list. Its step cannot be zero.</li>\n<li>A loop&#x27;s optional <code>else</code> runs when the loop finishes without <code>break</code> (including zero iterations).</li>\n<li>Avoid adding/removing elements from the same collection you are iterating over; build a new collection or iterate over a copy.</li>\n<li>One pass over <code>n</code> elements is O(n); all pairs are O(n\u00b2). Nested loops are not automatically quadratic: count actual iterations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>continue</code> before a <code>while</code> loop&#x27;s counter update can cause an infinite loop.</p>", "text": "\n```python\nfor value in values:                 # direct iteration\n    print(value)\nfor i, value in enumerate(values):    # index and value\n    print(i, value)\nfor left, right in zip(xs, ys):       # stops at shorter iterable\n    print(left, right)\n\nfor i in range(2, 8, 2):              # 2, 4, 6; stop excluded\n    print(i)\nfor i in range(3, -1, -1):            # 3, 2, 1, 0\n    print(i)\n\nwhile remaining > 0:\n    remaining -= 1                   # ensure progress toward termination\n```\n\n- `break`: exit the innermost loop. `continue`: skip to its next iteration. `pass`: do nothing.\n- `reversed(values)` iterates backward without making a reversed list.\n- `range` stores bounds/step rather than building a list. Its step cannot be zero.\n- A loop's optional `else` runs when the loop finishes without `break` (including zero iterations).\n- Avoid adding/removing elements from the same collection you are iterating over; build a new collection or iterate over a copy.\n- One pass over `n` elements is O(n); all pairs are O(n\u00b2). Nested loops are not automatically quadratic: count actual iterations.\n\n**Trap:** `continue` before a `while` loop's counter update can cause an infinite loop.\n\n", "minutes": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Strings", "html": "<p>Strings are immutable sequences. Indexing returns a one-character string; there is no separate character type.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>s = &quot;python&quot;\nlen(s)             # 6\ns[0], s[-1]        # (&#x27;p&#x27;, &#x27;n&#x27;)\ns[1:4]            # &#x27;yth&#x27;; start included, stop excluded\ns[::2]            # &#x27;pto&#x27;\ns[::-1]           # &#x27;nohtyp&#x27;\ns[99:]            # &#x27;&#x27;; slicing clips out-of-range bounds\n# s[99]           # IndexError\n# s[0] = &#x27;P&#x27;      # TypeError\ns = &quot;P&quot; + s[1:]    # creates a new string\nf&quot;{s}: {3.14159:.2f}&quot;  # &#x27;Python: 3.14&#x27;</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Iterate directly: <code>for ch in s</code>; use <code>enumerate(s)</code> when you need positions.</li>\n<li><code>s.split()</code> splits on whitespace and collapses repeated whitespace. <code>s.split(&quot;,&quot;)</code> uses a specific separator and preserves empty fields.</li>\n<li><code>&quot;-&quot;.join([&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;])</code> \u2192 <code>&quot;a-b&quot;</code>; all joined elements must be strings.</li>\n<li>Useful methods: <code>strip()</code>, <code>lower()</code>, <code>upper()</code>, <code>replace(old, new)</code>, <code>startswith(prefix)</code>, <code>endswith(suffix)</code>, <code>isalnum()</code>.</li>\n<li><code>s.find(&quot;x&quot;)</code> returns <code>-1</code> when absent; <code>s.index(&quot;x&quot;)</code> raises <code>ValueError</code>.</li>\n<li>Methods return new strings; <code>s.lower()</code> alone does not change <code>s</code>.</li>\n<li><code>ord(&quot;a&quot;)</code> \u2192 <code>97</code>; <code>chr(97)</code> \u2192 <code>&quot;a&quot;</code>. Confirm a problem&#x27;s character set before assuming only lowercase English letters.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Cost:</strong> indexing and <code>len</code> are O(1) in typical Python interview analysis; a slice of length k takes O(k) time/space. Repeatedly copying a growing string can take O(n\u00b2); accumulate pieces in a list and <code>&quot;&quot;.join(parts)</code> for linear total construction.</p>", "text": "\nStrings are immutable sequences. Indexing returns a one-character string; there is no separate character type.\n\n```python\ns = \"python\"\nlen(s)             # 6\ns[0], s[-1]        # ('p', 'n')\ns[1:4]            # 'yth'; start included, stop excluded\ns[::2]            # 'pto'\ns[::-1]           # 'nohtyp'\ns[99:]            # ''; slicing clips out-of-range bounds\n# s[99]           # IndexError\n# s[0] = 'P'      # TypeError\ns = \"P\" + s[1:]    # creates a new string\nf\"{s}: {3.14159:.2f}\"  # 'Python: 3.14'\n```\n\n- Iterate directly: `for ch in s`; use `enumerate(s)` when you need positions.\n- `s.split()` splits on whitespace and collapses repeated whitespace. `s.split(\",\")` uses a specific separator and preserves empty fields.\n- `\"-\".join([\"a\", \"b\"])` \u2192 `\"a-b\"`; all joined elements must be strings.\n- Useful methods: `strip()`, `lower()`, `upper()`, `replace(old, new)`, `startswith(prefix)`, `endswith(suffix)`, `isalnum()`.\n- `s.find(\"x\")` returns `-1` when absent; `s.index(\"x\")` raises `ValueError`.\n- Methods return new strings; `s.lower()` alone does not change `s`.\n- `ord(\"a\")` \u2192 `97`; `chr(97)` \u2192 `\"a\"`. Confirm a problem's character set before assuming only lowercase English letters.\n\n**Cost:** indexing and `len` are O(1) in typical Python interview analysis; a slice of length k takes O(k) time/space. Repeatedly copying a growing string can take O(n\u00b2); accumulate pieces in a list and `\"\".join(parts)` for linear total construction.\n\n", "minutes": 10}, {"id": "7", "title": "Lists and tuples", "html": "<p>Lists are mutable, ordered sequences; duplicates are allowed.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>nums = [3, 1, 2]\nnums.append(4)         # [3, 1, 2, 4]; adds one object\nnums.extend([5, 6])    # adds each element\nlast = nums.pop()     # removes and returns 6\nnums.insert(0, 9)     # inserts before index 0\nnums.remove(1)        # removes first matching value; ValueError if absent\nnums.index(2)         # first matching index; ValueError if absent\ncopy = nums[:]        # shallow copy, like nums.copy()\nsquares = [x * x for x in nums if x &gt; 2]</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Indexing/slicing follow the string rules, but list elements can be reassigned. <code>del nums[i]</code> removes by index.</li>\n<li><code>len</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>min</code>, <code>max</code> are useful. <code>min([])</code> and <code>max([])</code> raise <code>ValueError</code> unless supplied a <code>default</code>.</li>\n<li><code>nums.sort()</code> sorts in place and returns <code>None</code>; <code>sorted(nums)</code> returns a new list. Both accept <code>key=</code> and <code>reverse=</code>; sorting is stable.</li>\n<li><code>nums.reverse()</code> mutates and returns <code>None</code>; <code>nums[::-1]</code> creates a reversed copy.</li>\n<li><code>append([1, 2])</code> adds one nested list; <code>extend([1, 2])</code> adds two elements.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Critical traps:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>rows = [[0] * 3] * 2  # both entries reference the same row!\nrows = [[0] * 3 for _ in range(2)]  # independent rows\n\noriginal = [[1], [2]]\ncopy = original.copy()\ncopy[0].append(9)     # original also becomes [[1, 9], [2]]</code></pre>\n<p>A shallow copy separates the outer container, but shares nested objects. <code>copy.deepcopy</code> recursively copies nested structure when needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Tuples:</strong> <code>point = (2, 3)</code>; a single-element tuple is <code>(2,)</code>. Tuple slots cannot be reassigned, but a contained mutable object can still change. A tuple can be a dict key/set element only if all its elements are hashable.</p>", "text": "\nLists are mutable, ordered sequences; duplicates are allowed.\n\n```python\nnums = [3, 1, 2]\nnums.append(4)         # [3, 1, 2, 4]; adds one object\nnums.extend([5, 6])    # adds each element\nlast = nums.pop()     # removes and returns 6\nnums.insert(0, 9)     # inserts before index 0\nnums.remove(1)        # removes first matching value; ValueError if absent\nnums.index(2)         # first matching index; ValueError if absent\ncopy = nums[:]        # shallow copy, like nums.copy()\nsquares = [x * x for x in nums if x > 2]\n```\n\n- Indexing/slicing follow the string rules, but list elements can be reassigned. `del nums[i]` removes by index.\n- `len`, `sum`, `min`, `max` are useful. `min([])` and `max([])` raise `ValueError` unless supplied a `default`.\n- `nums.sort()` sorts in place and returns `None`; `sorted(nums)` returns a new list. Both accept `key=` and `reverse=`; sorting is stable.\n- `nums.reverse()` mutates and returns `None`; `nums[::-1]` creates a reversed copy.\n- `append([1, 2])` adds one nested list; `extend([1, 2])` adds two elements.\n\n**Critical traps:**\n\n```python\nrows = [[0] * 3] * 2  # both entries reference the same row!\nrows = [[0] * 3 for _ in range(2)]  # independent rows\n\noriginal = [[1], [2]]\ncopy = original.copy()\ncopy[0].append(9)     # original also becomes [[1, 9], [2]]\n```\n\nA shallow copy separates the outer container, but shares nested objects. `copy.deepcopy` recursively copies nested structure when needed.\n\n**Tuples:** `point = (2, 3)`; a single-element tuple is `(2,)`. Tuple slots cannot be reassigned, but a contained mutable object can still change. A tuple can be a dict key/set element only if all its elements are hashable.\n\n", "minutes": 12}, {"id": "8", "title": "Sets", "html": "<p>Sets store distinct hashable elements. They have no positional indexing or guaranteed iteration order.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>seen = set()             # {} creates a dict!\nseen.add(3)\nseen.update([3, 4])       # {3, 4}\nexists = 3 in seen\nseen.discard(9)          # no error if absent\nseen.remove(3)           # KeyError if absent\n\n{1, 2} | {2, 3}          # union: {1, 2, 3}\n{1, 2} &amp; {2, 3}          # intersection: {2}\n{1, 2} - {2, 3}          # difference: {1}\n{1, 2} ^ {2, 3}          # symmetric difference: {1, 3}\n{1} &lt;= {1, 2}            # subset: True</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Typical uses: membership, duplicate detection, visited nodes.</li>\n<li>Lists, dicts, and sets are unhashable. Strings, integers, and tuples of hashable values are suitable elements.</li>\n<li><code>set(values)</code> removes duplicates but does not preserve sequence order. <code>frozenset</code> is immutable and hashable.</li>\n</ul>", "text": "\nSets store distinct hashable elements. They have no positional indexing or guaranteed iteration order.\n\n```python\nseen = set()             # {} creates a dict!\nseen.add(3)\nseen.update([3, 4])       # {3, 4}\nexists = 3 in seen\nseen.discard(9)          # no error if absent\nseen.remove(3)           # KeyError if absent\n\n{1, 2} | {2, 3}          # union: {1, 2, 3}\n{1, 2} & {2, 3}          # intersection: {2}\n{1, 2} - {2, 3}          # difference: {1}\n{1, 2} ^ {2, 3}          # symmetric difference: {1, 3}\n{1} <= {1, 2}            # subset: True\n```\n\n- Typical uses: membership, duplicate detection, visited nodes.\n- Lists, dicts, and sets are unhashable. Strings, integers, and tuples of hashable values are suitable elements.\n- `set(values)` removes duplicates but does not preserve sequence order. `frozenset` is immutable and hashable.\n\n", "minutes": 7}, {"id": "9", "title": "Dictionaries", "html": "<p>Dictionaries map unique hashable keys to arbitrary values. Iteration follows insertion order, not sorted order. Assigning an existing key replaces its value.</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>counts = {&quot;a&quot;: 2}\ncounts[&quot;b&quot;] = 1\ncounts[&quot;a&quot;] += 1\ncounts.get(&quot;missing&quot;, 0)   # 0; does not insert the key\ncounts[&quot;c&quot;] = counts.get(&quot;c&quot;, 0) + 1\n\nfor key, value in counts.items():\n    print(key, value)\n\nremoved = counts.pop(&quot;b&quot;)\ncounts.pop(&quot;missing&quot;, None)  # default avoids KeyError\n# del counts[&#x27;a&#x27;]            # KeyError if absent</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>key in d</code> checks <strong>keys</strong>. <code>value in d.values()</code> checks values and takes a linear scan.</li>\n<li><code>d[key]</code> raises <code>KeyError</code> when absent. <code>get</code> is useful when absence is expected.</li>\n<li><code>keys()</code>, <code>values()</code>, and <code>items()</code> return dynamic views; use <code>list(...)</code> for a snapshot.</li>\n<li><code>d.update(other)</code> merges, overwriting duplicate keys. <code>d.copy()</code> is shallow.</li>\n<li>Do not change dict size while iterating over it.</li>\n<li><code>True</code> and <code>1</code> compare equal and have the same hash, so they represent the same dictionary key.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Useful interview additions:</p>\n<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>from collections import Counter, defaultdict\n\nfreq = Counter(&quot;banana&quot;)         # a:3, n:2, b:1; missing count is 0\npositions = defaultdict(list)\npositions[&quot;a&quot;].append(0)          # indexing missing key creates its list</code></pre>\n<p class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> <code>d.get(key)</code> returning <code>None</code> cannot distinguish a missing key from a stored <code>None</code>. Use <code>key in d</code> when that distinction matters.</p>", "text": "\nDictionaries map unique hashable keys to arbitrary values. Iteration follows insertion order, not sorted order. Assigning an existing key replaces its value.\n\n```python\ncounts = {\"a\": 2}\ncounts[\"b\"] = 1\ncounts[\"a\"] += 1\ncounts.get(\"missing\", 0)   # 0; does not insert the key\ncounts[\"c\"] = counts.get(\"c\", 0) + 1\n\nfor key, value in counts.items():\n    print(key, value)\n\nremoved = counts.pop(\"b\")\ncounts.pop(\"missing\", None)  # default avoids KeyError\n# del counts['a']            # KeyError if absent\n```\n\n- `key in d` checks **keys**. `value in d.values()` checks values and takes a linear scan.\n- `d[key]` raises `KeyError` when absent. `get` is useful when absence is expected.\n- `keys()`, `values()`, and `items()` return dynamic views; use `list(...)` for a snapshot.\n- `d.update(other)` merges, overwriting duplicate keys. `d.copy()` is shallow.\n- Do not change dict size while iterating over it.\n- `True` and `1` compare equal and have the same hash, so they represent the same dictionary key.\n\nUseful interview additions:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\n\nfreq = Counter(\"banana\")         # a:3, n:2, b:1; missing count is 0\npositions = defaultdict(list)\npositions[\"a\"].append(0)          # indexing missing key creates its list\n```\n\n**Trap:** `d.get(key)` returning `None` cannot distinguish a missing key from a stored `None`. Use `key in d` when that distinction matters.\n\n", "minutes": 10}, {"id": "10", "title": "Reading stdin", "html": "<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>name = input()                        # str, trailing newline removed\nn = int(input())                      # explicit conversion\nnums = list(map(int, input().split())) # space-separated integers\na, b = map(int, input().split())       # exactly two values required</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>input()</code> returns a string even when the input looks numeric. End-of-file raises <code>EOFError</code>.</li>\n<li><code>map</code> is a lazy iterator; use <code>list(...)</code> if you need indexing or repeated traversal.</li>\n<li>For larger input, <code>sys.stdin.readline()</code> keeps the newline and returns <code>&quot;&quot;</code> at EOF. Use <code>.rstrip(&quot;\\n&quot;)</code> if spaces must be preserved.</li>\n<li>Many live interview platforms call your function directly: follow the provided signature and return values. Parse stdin only when required.</li>\n<li><code>print(*nums)</code> prints elements separated by spaces; avoid extra debug output in judged submissions.</li>\n</ul>", "text": "\n```python\nname = input()                        # str, trailing newline removed\nn = int(input())                      # explicit conversion\nnums = list(map(int, input().split())) # space-separated integers\na, b = map(int, input().split())       # exactly two values required\n```\n\n- `input()` returns a string even when the input looks numeric. End-of-file raises `EOFError`.\n- `map` is a lazy iterator; use `list(...)` if you need indexing or repeated traversal.\n- For larger input, `sys.stdin.readline()` keeps the newline and returns `\"\"` at EOF. Use `.rstrip(\"\\n\")` if spaces must be preserved.\n- Many live interview platforms call your function directly: follow the provided signature and return values. Parse stdin only when required.\n- `print(*nums)` prints elements separated by spaces; avoid extra debug output in judged submissions.\n\n", "minutes": 5}, {"id": "11", "title": "Exception handling", "html": "<div class=\"code-label\">PYTHON</div><pre><code>def parse_integer(text):\n    try:\n        value = int(text)\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        raise ValueError(&quot;Expected an integer&quot;) from exc\n    else:\n        return value       # runs if try succeeds\n    finally:\n        pass               # cleanup on normal return or exception</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Catch specific expected exceptions; order handlers from specific to general.</li>\n<li>Use <code>except (ValueError, TypeError):</code> when handling both identically, or separate handlers for different recovery.</li>\n<li><code>raise</code> inside a handler re-raises the current exception. <code>raise ValueError(&quot;message&quot;)</code> signals invalid input.</li>\n<li>Avoid swallowing errors with bare <code>except:</code> or <code>except Exception: pass</code>; this hides defects.</li>\n<li>Avoid <code>return</code> in <code>finally</code>: it can override an earlier return or suppress an exception.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Exception</th><th>Typical trigger</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>TypeError</code></td><td>Unsupported operation/type; unhashable key</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>ValueError</code></td><td>Correct kind of argument, invalid value; failed conversion</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>IndexError</code></td><td>Sequence index outside bounds</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>KeyError</code></td><td>Missing dictionary key / set removal</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>NameError</code></td><td>Name not defined</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>UnboundLocalError</code></td><td>Local name read before assignment</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>ZeroDivisionError</code></td><td>Division or modulo by zero</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>", "text": "\n```python\ndef parse_integer(text):\n    try:\n        value = int(text)\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected an integer\") from exc\n    else:\n        return value       # runs if try succeeds\n    finally:\n        pass               # cleanup on normal return or exception\n```\n\n- Catch specific expected exceptions; order handlers from specific to general.\n- Use `except (ValueError, TypeError):` when handling both identically, or separate handlers for different recovery.\n- `raise` inside a handler re-raises the current exception. `raise ValueError(\"message\")` signals invalid input.\n- Avoid swallowing errors with bare `except:` or `except Exception: pass`; this hides defects.\n- Avoid `return` in `finally`: it can override an earlier return or suppress an exception.\n\n| Exception | Typical trigger |\n|---|---|\n| `TypeError` | Unsupported operation/type; unhashable key |\n| `ValueError` | Correct kind of argument, invalid value; failed conversion |\n| `IndexError` | Sequence index outside bounds |\n| `KeyError` | Missing dictionary key / set removal |\n| `NameError` | Name not defined |\n| `UnboundLocalError` | Local name read before assignment |\n| `ZeroDivisionError` | Division or modulo by zero |\n\n", "minutes": 7}, {"id": "12", "title": "Costs to remember before DSA", "html": "<p>Assume n elements and ordinary fixed-size values; hash-table costs are expected/average, with O(n) worst cases for individual lookups/updates.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>List index / assignment / <code>len</code></td><td>O(1)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>List append</td><td>O(1) amortized</td></tr>\n<tr><td>List pop from end</td><td>O(1)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>List insert/remove/pop at front</td><td>O(n)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>List membership / index / min / max / sum</td><td>O(n)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>List slice or shallow copy of k elements</td><td>O(k), also O(k) extra space</td></tr>\n<tr><td>List sorting</td><td>O(n log n) worst case</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dict/set membership, lookup, insertion, deletion</td><td>O(1) average; O(n) worst case</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Iterate list/dict/set</td><td>O(n)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Hashing/comparing long keys adds work; do not assume a newly created long string/tuple key costs O(1) to process.</p>", "text": "\nAssume n elements and ordinary fixed-size values; hash-table costs are expected/average, with O(n) worst cases for individual lookups/updates.\n\n| Operation | Time |\n|---|---|\n| List index / assignment / `len` | O(1) |\n| List append | O(1) amortized |\n| List pop from end | O(1) |\n| List insert/remove/pop at front | O(n) |\n| List membership / index / min / max / sum | O(n) |\n| List slice or shallow copy of k elements | O(k), also O(k) extra space |\n| List sorting | O(n log n) worst case |\n| Dict/set membership, lookup, insertion, deletion | O(1) average; O(n) worst case |\n| Iterate list/dict/set | O(n) |\n\nHashing/comparing long keys adds work; do not assume a newly created long string/tuple key costs O(1) to process.\n\n", "minutes": 6}, {"id": "13", "title": "Ready to move on", "html": "<p>You should be able to explain these without looking them up:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Assignment versus mutation versus shallow copying.</li>\n<li>Equality versus identity; truthiness versus checking for <code>None</code>.</li>\n<li>Floor division with negatives and short-circuit evaluation.</li>\n<li>Return values, local scope, and the mutable-default pitfall.</li>\n<li>Exclusive slice/range bounds and safe reverse iteration.</li>\n<li><code>append</code> versus <code>extend</code>; <code>sort</code> versus <code>sorted</code>.</li>\n<li>Hashability, dictionary membership, and missing-key handling.</li>\n<li>Why list membership and front removal can make an otherwise simple algorithm slow.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Next bridge topics: Big-O, recursion/call stacks, <code>deque</code>, <code>heapq</code>, <code>bisect</code>, and small node classes. These belong to the DSA phase rather than this basics revision.</p>", "text": "\nYou should be able to explain these without looking them up:\n\n- Assignment versus mutation versus shallow copying.\n- Equality versus identity; truthiness versus checking for `None`.\n- Floor division with negatives and short-circuit evaluation.\n- Return values, local scope, and the mutable-default pitfall.\n- Exclusive slice/range bounds and safe reverse iteration.\n- `append` versus `extend`; `sort` versus `sorted`.\n- Hashability, dictionary membership, and missing-key handling.\n- Why list membership and front removal can make an otherwise simple algorithm slow.\n\nNext bridge topics: Big-O, recursion/call stacks, `deque`, `heapq`, `bisect`, and small node classes. These belong to the DSA phase rather than this basics revision.\n", "minutes": 3}];
const SCHEDULE = "<h3>Interview prep schedule</h3>\n<p>Working assumption: 60\u201375 minutes a day, five focused days per week, one light review day, and one rest/buffer day. Use relative weeks until an interview date is confirmed. This plan focuses on Python and general coding interviews; ML theory, ML system design, and role-specific rounds need a separate track once the format is known.</p>\n<h3>Week 1 \u2014 Python revision now</h3>\n<p>Use Python revision notes, based on your screenshot topics.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Content</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>Variables, references, types, casting; arithmetic and Boolean operators</td><td>45\u201360 min</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>Functions, return values, scope, defaults; conditionals and truthiness</td><td>60 min</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>Loops; strings, indexing, slicing, immutability</td><td>60 min</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>Lists, tuples, aliasing, shallow copies, sorting</td><td>60\u201375 min</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>Sets, dictionaries, hashability, operation costs</td><td>60\u201375 min</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6</td><td>Stdin and exceptions; reread marked weak spots</td><td>30\u201345 min</td></tr>\n<tr><td>7</td><td>Rest / buffer</td><td>\u2014</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>For each revision session: 5 minutes recalling yesterday&#x27;s concepts, 30\u201340 minutes reading and tracing examples, then 10\u201315 minutes explaining the key rules aloud and recording weak spots. Optional: type a worked example from memory, then compare it. No problem sets or timed questions in this first phase.</p>\n<p>Move on when you can explain the notes&#x27; readiness checklist without prompting. If needed, repeat only the weak sections rather than restarting the whole week.</p>\n<h3>Weeks 2\u20136 \u2014 DSA progression</h3>\n<p>The following is the roadmap; detailed notes and question selection come after the Python revision. Within each topic, revise the concept and implementation first, then introduce practice.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Week</th><th>Fundamentals to revise</th><th>Practice phase, when ready</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>Big-O time/space; arrays and strings; hash maps/sets; sorting; prefix sums</td><td>Arrays/hashing, two pointers, sliding windows</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>Stack, queue/<code>deque</code>, linked lists, binary search and boundaries</td><td>Stack problems, linked-list operations, binary search</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>Recursion and call stack; tree traversals; BSTs; heaps/priority queues</td><td>DFS/BFS on trees, BST operations, top-k</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>Graph representations; visited sets; BFS/DFS; backtracking; topological sorting</td><td>Grid/graph traversal, subsets/permutations, dependencies</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6</td><td>Intervals and greedy reasoning; DP states/transitions/base cases; basic bit operations</td><td>Introductory 1D/2D DP, mixed review, mock interviews</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>For a five-day DSA week: days 1\u20132 cover concepts and worked implementations, days 3\u20134 cover selected problems, day 5 revisits mistakes and mixes earlier topics. Day 6 is light recall; day 7 is a buffer. Week 6 should reserve two sessions for mocks. Treat this as an initial pass, not a guarantee of mastery; extend weak topics as needed.</p>\n<p>A later practice session can use: 10 minutes recall, 15 minutes concept review, 30\u201340 minutes independent coding, 10 minutes explanation and mistake review. Start with one carefully reviewed problem per session rather than chasing a problem count.</p>\n<h3>Rebuild independent fluency</h3>\n<p>During recall and later practice, close coding agents and autocomplete. Use assistance after your own attempt to review reasoning and identify gaps. Revisit each weak concept the next day, about three days later, and the following week.</p>\n<p>For each future problem, explain the approach before coding, state the invariant and complexity, then trace empty/single-element inputs, duplicates, and boundary cases. Record the specific mistake and the rule that prevents it.</p>\n<h3>If the interview becomes urgent</h3>\n<p>For roughly two weeks of lead time: use 2\u20133 days for Python, then prioritize Big-O, arrays/hashing, two pointers/sliding windows, binary search, stacks/queues, and tree/graph traversal. Reserve the final two sessions for mocks and reviewing mistakes. Add heaps, backtracking, and DP according to the interview format and remaining time.</p>\n<p>Start today with Week 1, Day 1. The immediate deliverable is confident recall of Python behavior; question practice begins after that revision.</p>";
